<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cavalry warfare</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cavalry warfare is the use of mounted troops in battle, for shock charges, archery, scouting and rapid pursuit. Mounted mobility gave a recurring advantage to states that could breed horses and train riders, allowing swift conquest of large territories and domination of settled neighbours. The pattern is seen in steppe powers such as the Mongol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> empires, in the horse-archer armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and in the cavalry of West African states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>.</p></div>
